  "text": "The first step in keeping a forest productive over many years is to make sure there is enough land to provide the wood needed for a processing plant throughout an entire growth cycle. Simply applying forest‑care techniques to a plot is useless unless a sizable stock of trees exists to support future harvests and the capacity to grow the desired volume is present. In one northern province, officials have recently set the growth cycle for a common soft‑wood species at about 75 years. For commercial use, a standing‑timber reserve equal to fifty times the yearly harvest is considered sufficient, taking into account young stands and areas being replanted that currently hold little wood but will mature over the cycle. Large pulp and paper factories each use roughly 200,000–300,000 stacked‑wood units annually, implying a need for 10–15 million such units overall, which may require several million acres. The crucial question, then, is how much that land is worth. Understanding the economic value of timberland—whether for purchase or accounting—forms the financial base for estimating costs of fire protection, silvicultural work, and other management actions. This valuation must start from the role of forest products in meeting human needs and how different tree species and locations contribute to that role."
